nd{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-40 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-40 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378067 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378068 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378069 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378070 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378071 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378334 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-8)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-8)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378336 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378337 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378338 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378339 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378340 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378341 :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186378341 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{33}=2,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=4.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=2,{u}_{33}=2,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378348 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ then\n${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ each contain a row consisting only of zeros, so they are not invertible. However,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis invertible. So, $S$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, if $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix that is not invertible then $det(A)=0$ and therefore for all $\\alpha \\in R$ \n\n$$\ndet(\\alpha A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot det(A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $S$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $S$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ then\n${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ each contain a row consisting only of zeros, so they are not invertible. However,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis invertible. So, $S$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, if $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix that is not invertible then $det(A)=0$ and therefore for all $\\alpha \\in R$ \n\n$$\ndet(\\alpha A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot det(A)={\\alpha }^{2}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $S$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $S$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186378490 :question/problem "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+v+6 \\\\ 7v-2u+6\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u+v-2 \\\\ 4+4u+2v\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+9 \\\\ 8u+7v+6\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v+6 \\\\ 7v-2u+6\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{2}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2u+v-2 \\\\ 4+4u+2v\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{3}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+9 \\\\ 8u+7v+6\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186378490 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v+6 \\\\ 7v-2u+6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ -2u+7v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}|=7\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2)=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+v-2 \\\\ 4+4u+2v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+v \\\\ 4u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+9 \\\\ 8u+7v+6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ 8u+7v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}|=7\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 8=7.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{3}$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only.\"" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v+6 \\\\ 7v-2u+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+v \\\\ -2u+7v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{vmatrix}=7\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2)=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+v-2 \\\\ 4+4u+2v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+v \\\\ 4u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u+9 \\\\ 8u+7v+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ 8u+7v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}=7\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 8=7.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{3}$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186378497 :question/problem "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}6u+3v+3 \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}1-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v-2\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v-7\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}6u+3v+3 \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{2}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}1-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v-2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{3}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v-7\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186378497 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6u+3v+3 \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6u+3v \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}|=5\\cdot 6-3\\cdot 10=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=4\\cdot (-3)-(-4)\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v-7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}|=7\\cdot (-6)-(-4)\\cdot 8=-10\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{3}$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{3}$ only.\"" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+3v+3 \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6u+3v \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{vmatrix}=5\\cdot 6-3\\cdot 10=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=4\\cdot (-3)-(-4)\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v-7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}=7\\cdot (-6)-(-4)\\cdot 8=-10\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{3}$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{3}$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186378504 :question/problem "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-2u-5v-9 \\\\ 4u+9v-9\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3u+3v+4 \\\\ 3u+3v-1\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3u+v \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-2u-5v-9 \\\\ 4u+9v-9\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{2}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+3v+4 \\\\ 3u+3v-1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{3}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3u+v \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186378504 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2u-5v-9 \\\\ 4u+9v-9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2u-5v \\\\ 4u+9v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}|=9\\cdot (-2)-(-5)\\cdot 4=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+3v+4 \\\\ 3u+3v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+3v \\\\ 3u+3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+v \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+v \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ only.\"" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2u-5v-9 \\\\ 4u+9v-9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2u-5v \\\\ 4u+9v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{vmatrix}=9\\cdot (-2)-(-5)\\cdot 4=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+3v+4 \\\\ 3u+3v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+3v \\\\ 3u+3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+v \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+v \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186378511 :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a \\\\ b & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a \\\\ b & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378511 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1\\cdot 1-6\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-6+0+9=3.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1\\cdot 1-6\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-6+0+9=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378518 :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 2 \\\\ b & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 2 \\\\ b & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378518 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-9+5+(-6)=-10.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-9+5+(-6)=-10.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378525 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3v-7u+7 \\\\ v-2u+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7u+3v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-7)-3\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-7 \\\\ y-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1\\cdot (x-7)+3\\cdot (y-3) \\\\ -2\\cdot (x-7)+7\\cdot (y-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-x+3y-2 \\\\ -2x+7y-7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=(-1)x+3y-2,\\,v=-2x+7y+(-7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-1+3+(-7)=-5.\n$$" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3v-7u+7 \\\\ v-2u+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7u+3v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-7)-3\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-7 \\\\ y-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\cdot (x-7)+3\\cdot (y-3) \\\\ -2\\cdot (x-7)+7\\cdot (y-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-x+3y-2 \\\\ -2x+7y-7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=(-1)x+3y-2,\\,v=-2x+7y+(-7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-1+3+(-7)=-5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378643 :question/problem "If $S=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{aligned}]$, then ${S}^{3}=$" "If $S=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${S}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186378643 :question/worked-solution "Computing ${S}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =S\\cdot S \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${S}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $S$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${S}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{3} & ={S}^{2}\\cdot S \\\\  & =O\\cdot S \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Computing ${S}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =S\\cdot S \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${S}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $S$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${S}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{3} & ={S}^{2}\\cdot S \\\\  & =O\\cdot S \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378645 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378646 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}200 & 100 \\\\ 400 & 200\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}200 & 100 \\\\ 400 & 200\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378647 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}200 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 200\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}200 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 200\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378648 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}75 & 75 \\\\ 150 & 150\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}75 & 75 \\\\ 150 & 150\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378649 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}125 & 100 \\\\ 100 & 125\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}125 & 100 \\\\ 100 & 125\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378650 :question/problem "If $R=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{aligned}]$, then ${R}^{4}=$" "If $R=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${R}^{4}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186378650 :question/worked-solution "Computing ${R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & =R\\cdot R \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${R}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $R$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${R}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & ={R}^{2}\\cdot {R}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{R}^{4}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Computing ${R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & =R\\cdot R \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 25 \\\\ -9 & 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${R}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $R$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${R}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2} & ={R}^{2}\\cdot {R}^{2} \\\\  & =O\\cdot O \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{R}^{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378652 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}60 & 50 \\\\ 50 & 60\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}60 & 50 \\\\ 50 & 60\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378653 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378654 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}150 & 250 \\\\ 90 & 150\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}150 & 250 \\\\ 90 & 150\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378655 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -9 \\\\ 15 & -15\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -9 \\\\ 15 & -15\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378656 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378657 :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=14$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=14$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378664 :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.55 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.45 & 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of footwear is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}700\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}700\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.55 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.45 & 0.3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.595 \\\\ 0.405\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $40.5%$ of customers will purchase sports footwear. Computing $40.5%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.405=405\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $405\\,000$ customers will purchase sports footwear in one year." "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.55 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.45 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of footwear is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}700\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}\\begin{bmatrix}700\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.55 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.45 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.595 \\\\ 0.405\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $40.5%$ of customers will purchase sports footwear. Computing $40.5%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.405=405\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $405\\,000$ customers will purchase sports footwear in one year."] [:db/cas 17592186378671 :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.8\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186378671 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.1+0.9=1 \\\\ 0.2+0.8=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are non-negative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.5+0+0.5=1 \\\\ 0+1+0=1 \\\\ 0.5+0+0.5=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only.\"" "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 1.1 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.8\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\begin{aligned}0.1+0.9=1 \\\\ 0.2+0.8=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are non-negative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 & 0 & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\begin{aligned}0.5+0+0.5=1 \\\\ 0+1+0=1 \\\\ 0.5+0+0.5=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186378678 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each month: \n\n- $40%$ of housekeepers who used oil $A$ swap to oil $B.$ This implies that $60%$ of them remain using oil $A.$\n\n- $30%$ of housekeepers who used oil $B$ swap to oil $A.$ This implies that $70%$ of them remain using oil $B.$\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Oil $A$** | **Oil $B$** |\n| To: | **Oil $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Oil $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We are given the following situation for each month: \n\n- $40%$ of housekeepers who used oil $A$ swap to oil $B.$ This implies that $60%$ of them remain using oil $A.$\n\n- $30%$ of housekeepers who used oil $B$ swap to oil $A.$ This implies that $70%$ of them remain using oil $B.$\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Oil $A$** | **Oil $B$** |\n| To: | **Oil $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Oil $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378691 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -2{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -2{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-2.$ But before we conclude that $-2$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3(0)^{2}-2(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-2.$" "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -2{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -2{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-2.$ But before we conclude that $-2$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3(0)^{2}-2(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378698 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-2.\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378705 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find the value of $\\frac{{r}_{22}}{{q}_{12}}.$" "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find the value of $\\frac{{r}_{22}}{{q}_{12}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378705 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{1}{3})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+(\\frac{1}{3})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{r}_{22}}{{q}_{12}}=\\frac{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}})}{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}})}=2.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{1}{3})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+(\\frac{1}{3})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{r}_{22}}{{q}_{12}}=\\frac{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}})}{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}})}=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378759 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 6{x}_{1}^{2},\\,4{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 6{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 6{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=6(1)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 6.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $6.$ But before we conclude that $6$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=6.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3(0)^{2}+6(1)^{2}+4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of  $Q(x)$ is equal to $6.$" "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 6{x}_{1}^{2},\\,4{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 6{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 6{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=6(1)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 6.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $6.$ But before we conclude that $6$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=6.$\n\nNotice that when $x={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3(0)^{2}+6(1)^{2}+4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of  $Q(x)$ is equal to $6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378766 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -6{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -6{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=-6(1)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -6.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-6.$ But before we conclude that $-6$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-6.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(0)^{2}-6(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-6.$" "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -6{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -6{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=-6(1)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -6.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-6.$ But before we conclude that $-6$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-6.$\n\nNotice that when $x={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(0)^{2}-6(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378955 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -17 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-20)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -17 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]+20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}19 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 27\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-20)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -17 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-20)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -17 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}19 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 27\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-20)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378957 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378958 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378959 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378960 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378961 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378962 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}36-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (36-\\lambda )(36-\\lambda )-144 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-72\\lambda +1\\,152 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda -24) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=24.\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}36-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (36-\\lambda )(36-\\lambda )-144 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-72\\lambda +1\\,152 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda -24) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=24.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378969 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=2$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -54 \\\\ -36 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}36 & -108 \\\\ -108 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-72,$ we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{min}=-72.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-72:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-72)I & =[\\begin{aligned}36 & -108 \\\\ -108 & 36\\end{aligned}]+72[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}108 & -108 \\\\ -108 & 108\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-72)I)x=0$ gives the  eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=2$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -54 \\\\ -36 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -108 \\\\ -108 & 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-72,$ we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{min}=-72.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-72:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-72)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -108 \\\\ -108 & 36\\end{bmatrix}+72\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}108 & -108 \\\\ -108 & 108\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-72)I)x=0$ gives the  eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186378971 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378972 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378973 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378974 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186378975 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379369 :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n