$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=5v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=5.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379402 :question/problem "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T(v)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186379402 :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{2}{5}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{2}{5}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379444 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379444 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379478 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379478 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (1-t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot t+1\\cdot (1-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot t+(-1)\\cdot (1-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ (-t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+1\\cdot (-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+(-1)\\cdot (-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+t-1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t+1)^{′} \\\\ (-t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot (t+1)+1\\cdot (-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot (t+1)+(-1)\\cdot (-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =|\\begin{aligned}t & t+1 \\\\ 1-t & -t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =t\\cdot (-t)-(t+1)\\cdot (1-t) \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}-1+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,c}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(t)^{′} \\\\ (1-t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1-t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot t+1\\cdot (1-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot t+(-1)\\cdot (1-t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({t}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ (-t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+1\\cdot (-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+(-1)\\cdot (-t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+t-1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(t+1)^{′} \\\\ (-t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot (t+1)+1\\cdot (-t) \\\\ -1\\cdot (t+1)+(-1)\\cdot (-t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t & t+1 \\\\ 1-t & -t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t\\cdot (-t)-(t+1)\\cdot (1-t) \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}-1+{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,c}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system."] [:db/cas 17592186379803 :question/problem "Find ${M}^{32},$ where\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" "Find ${M}^{32},$ where\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379803 :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{32} & =P{D}^{32}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{32}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{32} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[32]{3})^{32}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-29 & 48 \\\\ -20 & 33\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{32} & =P{D}^{32}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[32]{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{32}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{32} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[32]{3})^{32}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 9 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-29 & 48 \\\\ -20 & 33\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186379805 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-29 & 48 \\\\ -20 & 33\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-29 & 48 \\\\ -20 & 33\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379806 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379807 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & 24 \\\\ -20 & 12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & 24 \\\\ -20 & 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379808 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -29 \\\\ 11 & -12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -29 \\\\ 11 & -12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379809 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 & 3 \\\\ 32 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 3 \\\\ 32 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379810 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186379810 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-1I)\\text{x}=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-1I)\\text{x}=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."] [:db/cas 17592186379812 :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379813 :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379814 :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" "D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379815 :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379818 :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}7{e}^{7t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}7{e}^{7t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379819 :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379820 :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{7t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{7t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379821 :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{7t} \\\\ 7{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{7t} \\\\ 7{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379822 :answer/value "Aw=[\\begin{aligned}14{e}^{7t} \\\\ 15{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" "Aw=\\begin{bmatrix}14{e}^{7t} \\\\ 15{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186379994 :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=1 \\\\ 0x+0y=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=1$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=1,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=1 \\\\ 0x+0y=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=1$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=1,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution."] [:db/cas 17592186379996 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186379997 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-3y \\\\ y\