\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186386591 :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}" "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}"] [:db/cas 17592186386592 :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186386592 :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=-6 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 18 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 18 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{aligned}] &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second row of the RREF matrix above, we see that the system of equations has no solution.\n\nTherefore, ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=-6 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 18 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 18 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second row of the RREF matrix above, we see that the system of equations has no solution.\n\nTherefore, ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186386599 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186386599 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+5=8.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+5=8.$"] [:db/cas 17592186386606 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186386606 :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=3I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=3I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 1