corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4-3\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$"] [:db/cas 17592186388396 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186388398 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -7 & -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -7 & -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388399 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -7 & -6 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -7 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -7 & -6 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388400 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & -7\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -7 & -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186388401 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & 